  "readmeFile": "docs\\README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Features\n- Added support for PDF/A-4, PDF/A-4E, and PDF/A-4F (ISO 19005-4:2020) compliance.\n- Added support for signing PDFs with a Hardware Security Module (HSM) via USB token.\nEnhancements\n- Improved memory usage in the form generation flow. PDF-1599\nBug Fixes\n- Fixed an issue where the header and footer were in an invalid location when applied to rotated landscape pages.\n- Fixed an issue where converting certain PDFs to PDF/A 1a/1b failed validation due to an invalid CIDSet in subsetted fonts.\n- Fixed an issue where IronPDF could not access a temporary file directory with Nordic characters.\n- Fixed an issue where CreatePdfFormsFromHtml created incorrect form field names.\n- Fixed a critical issue where ASP.NET Framework web applications would freeze completely when validating licenses.\n- Fixed an issue where an \"Unsupported platform\" error was thrown in some Linux environments.",
  "requireLicenseAcceptance": true,
  "summary": "IronPDF helps C# Software Engineers to create, edit and extract PDF content in .NET projects.\n\n# C# HTML to PDF for .NET 10, .NET 9, .NET 8, .NET 7, .NET 6, .NET 5, Core, Standard, and Framework\n# Work with PDFs in C# using HTML, MVC, ASPX, and images\n# Generate, Edit, Read and Secure PDF Documents\n\nGenerate PDFs with Pixel Perfect Chrome HTML to PDF Rendering:\nThe Iron PDF library takes the frustration out of generating PDF documents by not relying on proprietary APIs. “Html-To-Pdf” renders pixel-perfect PDFs from open standard document types: HTML, JS, CSS, JPG, PNG, GIF, and SVG. In short, it uses the skills that developers already possess.\n\nFully Supports:\n* .NET 10, .NET 9, .NET 8, .NET 7, .NET 6, .NET 5, Framework, Core, and Standard\n* Windows, MacOS, Linux, Azure, Docker and AWS\n\nFor code examples, tutorials and documentation visit https://ironpdf.com/",
